<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qatr al-Nada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 900</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qatr al-Nada was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a>, the Tulunid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Her marriage to the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a>, agreed in 892 and celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, sealed the peace between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> and the caliphate. Her dowry and trousseau were so lavish that they became legendary in Arabic literature and helped exhaust the Tulunid treasury. She died young, around 900, in Baghdad.</p></div>
